    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.ai\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.13 (!at least latest Nightly build!)\n\n### Description\n\n`:help treesitter-defaults` shows that there are keybindings for incremental selection in neovim core, namely `an` and `in` in visual and operator-pending modes. However, [mini.ai](http://mini.ai/) seems to inhibit using them. Typing `va` (i.e. entering visual mode, then pressing `a`), eventually brings the [mini.ai](http://mini.ai/) hint which reminds me to choose a text object id, but it doesn't accept `n` as a valid input, and simply ignores that letter. Pressing `van` quickly, without waiting for the hint, obviously doesn't work either.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nminimal `init.lua` (using vim.pack 😁)\n\n```lua\nvim.pack.add({'https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim'})\nrequire(\"mini.ai\").setup()\n```\n\nand a basic file to test on\n\n```lua\nlocal a = function()\n\tlocal b = {\n\t\tc = 1\n\t}\nend\n```\n\nPutting the cursor on `c` and typing `van` in normal mode starts incremental selection when using `nvim --clean test.lua`. Using the minimal config gives a mini.ai reminder.",
